## Answer (3)

### General Comments

Generally, when you refactor a code module you want to better components with a clear responsibilities. Components can be interpreted as objects (class cluster), functions (methods), etc.

One important aspect of software design is to compose or break down complex tasks into smaller more manageable component parts. Ideally, these components are *reusable*, *extensible* and *can be tested in isolation*.

### Chained async task execution, Promise-style

For your problem, you essentially want to execute a bunch of asynchronous tasks sequentially. This can be achieved using different patterns. The general extensible way is to build a "Pipe and Filter" architecture.

An asynchronous function can be seen as a "filter". The pipe ensures elements (typically data computed by the former task) passed from output to input.

I'm assuming Promises, since they are trivial to setup and use and they are *extensible*.

In Objective-C, Promises are available here for example: Objective-C Promises (rename to RxPromise).

### Implementation

The following code first prints 42, then 21,and then 43:

```
- (void) testChainedPromise
{
    Promise* promise = [Promise new];
    NSLog(@"42: %p", promise);
    [promise.then:^id(Promise* x){return nil;}
        onQueue:dispatch_get_global_queue(DISPATCH_QUEUE_PRIORITY_DEFAULT,0)];

    Promise* promise2 = [promise.then:^id(Promise* x){
        // task 1: complex async function, like api request "getFeatureDataAsyncWithCompletion:"
        // in this simple example, just return a number
        return @42;
    } onQueue:dispatch_get_global_queue(DISPATCH_QUEUE_PRIORITY_DEFAULT, 0) ];

    Promise* promise3 = [promise2 .then:^id(Promise* x){
        // task 2: execute complex async function which requires the results from task 1

        NSInteger anInteger = [x value];
        NSLog(@"21: %p", promise3);

        // in this simple example, just return (@21 + anInteger)
        return @(21 + anInteger);
    } onQueue:dispatch_get_global_queue(DISPATCH_QUEUE_PRIORITY_DEFAULT, 0) ];

    // ... and so on
    [[promise3 then:^id(Promise* x){
        NSInteger anInteger = [x value];
        NSLog(@"43: %p", promise3);

        // in this simple example, just return (@21 + anInteger)
        return @(21 + anInteger);
    } onQueue:dispatch_get_global_queue(DISPATCH_QUEUE_PRIORITY_DEFAULT, 0) ]
     then:^id(Promise* x, NSError* error) {
        if (x) {
            NSInteger anInteger = [x value];
            NSLog(@"then: %ld", (long)anInteger);
            return nil;
        }
        else if (error) {
            NSLog(@"error: %@", error);
            return nil;
        }
        // error will be nil when promise is cancelled
        else {
            NSLog(@"Promise has been cancelled.");
            return nil;
        }
    }];
}
```

When adapting this approach to your code above, you basically extract *every* asynchronous function (API request) and obtain a `Promise*` by calling `[promise.then: ... ]` and so on.

This very "imperative style" leads to "dangling" temporary Promises. Utilities can extract them into a more functional style which utilizes "higher order functions" or "Plans".

Look here for an example: `Plan`

```
- (Promise*) obtainPlanExample {
    NSError* error = nil;
    Plan* plan = [Plan new];

    [plan then:^id(Promise* ignore1) {
        NSLog(@"task1: start");
        return [self task1WithCompletion:^(id x, NSError* e){
            NSLog(@"task1: finished");
            if (e) {
                error = e;
                return nil;
            }
            return x;
        }];
    } then:^id(Promise* ignore2) {
        NSLog(@"task2: start");
        return [self task2WithCompletion:^(id x, NSError* e){
            NSLog(@"task2: finished");
            if (e) {
                error = e;
                return nil;
            }
            return x;
        }];
    } then:^id(Promise* ignore3) {
        NSLog(@"task3: start");
        return [self task3WithCompletion:^(id x, NSError* e){
            NSLog(@"task3: finished");
            if (e) {
                error = e;
                return nil;
            }
            return x;
        }];
    } ];

    return [plan execute:0 error:&error];
}
```

You can wrap your *every* asynchronous function as follows into an asynchronous function which returns a `Promise*`. Notable is, that the completion handler gets a `Promise*`. That completion handler simply "fulfills" the Promise - which makes it the asynchronous result of the function:

```
- (Promise*) doAnAPICallWithCompletion:(void (^)(id, NSError*))completion {
    return promiseForAPIGetFeatureDataAsyncWithCompletion:^(BOOL succeeded, id response, NSError *error) {
        void (^fulfill)(Promise*, NSError*) = ^(Promise* p, NSError* e){
            if (completion) {
                completion(response, error);
            }
            if (e) {
                [p rejectWithReason:e];
            }
            else {
                [p fulfillWithValue:response];
            }
        };
        if (succeeded) {
            fulfill(nil, error);
        }
        else {
            fulfill(nil, [NSError errorWithDomain:...]);
        }
    }];
}
```

And this is a utility which wraps any completion handler-based asynchronous API function into a function which returns a Promise:

```
- (Promise*) promiseForAPIGetFeatureDataAsyncWithCompletion:
    (void (^)(BOOL succeeded, id response, NSError *error))completion {
    Promise* promise = [Promise new];
    dispatch_async(dispatch_get_global_queue(DISPATCH_QUEUE_PRIORITY_DEFAULT,0), ^{
        completion(YES, nil, nil);
    });
    return promise;
}
```

### Other design issues

This "Pipe and Filter" architecture - where every filter (function) gets its input from the pipe and emits its output to the pipe, and where *control* is passed along with the token - is a well known and proven pattern. It nicely scales into "dataflow" architecture. There are a lot of benefits, for example:

- quickly adapt to changes simply by removing or adding filters (functions) or modify where to get input (URL and API parameters)
- code which is easier to maintain and test

Since you've got a "Node" along the "pipe" which is, lets say "special" - as far as I understood your post, you will get a token from the server ("session_id") which must be used in subsequent calls. That means, that node needs to *know* about this session concept, and how to feed it into the pipe so that the subsequent node can "extract" it from the pipe.

This can be achieved by using appropriate data "containers". For example, an object or a dictionary.

E.g. as you "inject" a dictionary where the first N entries contain tokens used in subsequent requests, and the last entry (which is defined as so) will contain the results of the final request.

Zipping" input and output is another possibility:

We can model the input as a *tuple* `<request, input>` and the output as a *tuple* `<request, output>`.

The original input thus is modeled as:

<"login","user" + "pwd">

A subsequent request after having obtained a "session_id" would look like this:

<"getFeatureDataAsync","session_id">

The results would be then also returned as tuple. For example:

<"getFeatureDataAsync","features">

An element of the pipe could then look like this:

```
Stream<Tuple<Request,Input,Output>>
```

where `Request` is a Class cluster, `Input` and `Output` just define the "interface".

Each element of the pipe which can be worked by a node, represents a "closed function" where the input is just the value `Input` and the output is just the value `Output`, the request doesn't "matter" - that's totally the raison d'etre of the node and fully conceptually separated from actual input and output.

